    "description": "The order service of a commerce platform has a test suite of 240 tests, of which 90 hit a real PostgreSQL and a staging payment provider. They fail on a developer laptop, take 11 minutes in CI and are flaky at month end. The storage and payment collaborators must be replaced so that tests assert what the service asked for (which payment intent, with which amount, in which order) and can force the error paths (declined card, duplicate submit) deterministically, while a handful of tests should still run against the real integration. The team also wants the replacement to fail loudly when the service calls an unexpected collaborator method. The decision to implement: how to stand in for a collaborator during a test so calls can be programmed, observed and verified without touching the real system.",
    "decision_statement": "Stand in for collaborators in tests so calls can be programmed, observed and verified without the real system.",
    "notes": "Holdout family: stub/spy are the near misses; fake is acceptable where behaviour matters more than calls."
